/**
\page platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service ADR-040: Site role (ONLINE/STANDBY) as an RTE Redundancy Service

\section platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_status Status

**Accepted (2026-10-02); steps 1 to 4 done; step 5 in progress (5a, 5b-1, 5b-2 and 5b-3a done, 5b-3b and 5c
open).** How the service behaves as built is in the section "Service behaviour as built" below; the end-to-end system
view (both sites, A and B, cross-compare, checkpoint) is RBC_GP's
[Site redundancy and A/B agreement](https://github.com/bula173/RBC_GP/blob/master/docs/STANDBY_AND_SITE_LINK_ARCHITECTURE.dox). Plan for gap G1 of the root
`docs/architecture/CAPELLA_ARCHITECTURE_BASELINE.dox`. Decisions 1 and 2 below were taken by the product owner on
2026-10-02 (the model overrides A3; drive points stay in the application cycle). Step 1 moved the pure policy
(`rte_site_role_policy`, REQ-SITEROLE-001..004). Step 2 moved the mechanism: `rte_site_role` owns the inter-site
link, the startup negotiation, frame exchange, partner supervision and teardown, the takeover confirmation, the
warm-standby sync and the FAULTED flush (REQ-SITEROLE-010..013, `tests/site_role/test_rte_site_role.c`). RBC_GP's
`ab_gp_channel_negotiate.c` is now the Site Role Client: state codec (sessions, db), what a promotion takes over
(transfer policy), local facts. Wire layout and log text unchanged. Two seams are still the application's: the pause between startup connect
attempts (`delay` callback - the OAL has no sleep service) and the console line sink (`trace` callback). Step 3
(2026-10-03) moved the A/B role agreement rule and its 3-strike count: `rte_site_role_check_sibling()`
(REQ-SITEROLE-014) logs the mismatch lines and the `NEGOTIATION_MISMATCH` event and returns a verdict; GP forwards
the sibling's site-state frame and keeps the fault reaction (safe-state reboot). Step 4a (2026-10-03): the token
check and the challenge are in `rte_site_role` (REQ-SITEROLE-015, decision 4 below). Steps 4b-4e (2026-10-03) are
done with no design change: RBC_GP wires the key and the token builder, RBC_Maintenance_Web is the interim issuer,
RBC_Test_Env covers the takeover and its negative cases, and the documentation is updated. Step 5a (2026-10-06,
RBC_GP only): A and B of a site agree their startup timestamp over the A/B peer link (`PEER_MSG_KIND_STARTUP_TS`);
the shared `/tmp` file is gone. Step 5b-1 (2026-10-06): the sibling-state source seam (REQ-SITEROLE-016). Step 5b-2
(2026-10-06, RBC_GP): GP stores the sibling's raw site-state frame and hands it to the service through the source;
the service derives the sibling facts and runs the A/B check, GP only reacts to `rte_site_role_sibling_verdict()`.
Step 5b-3a (2026-10-06): a channel starting next to a running A/B sibling resumes the sibling's role
(REQ-SITEROLE-017); GP does not deliver the frame at start-up yet (5b-3b), so in RBC_GP the start-up still runs the
timestamp tie-break. Step 5c (site and peer links on `rte_flow`, GP copies deleted, `rte_site_role_check_sibling()`
removed from the API) is open.

\section platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_context Context

The Capella model (RBC-GP-ARCH-CAP 0.1, 2026-10-02) puts site-role negotiation, partner supervision, verification of the
dispatcher's takeover confirmation and state transfer on promotion in RTE **Redundancy Services** ("[SF] Negotiate Site
Role", "[SF] Transfer State on Promotion"). The application keeps only a declarative **Site Role Client** (IF-05):
it receives the role, supplies readiness and the state to replicate, and passes the confirmation on. Role state is
exchanged independently by channels A and B and the role decision must agree in both (2oo2); two ONLINE sites are
excluded.

On 2026-10-02 all of it was in RBC_GP (`AB/GP/com/`); the table is kept as the starting point of the move:

| Piece | Where | Generic (to RTE) or application (stays) |
|---|---|---|
| West-East link: raw `rte_netlink`, fixed A/B ports, reconnect bounded to 50 ms | `ab_gp_channel_negotiate.c` | generic |
| Startup tie-break (older timestamp wins), wrapping `rte_dual_channel` + `rte_dual_negotiator` | same | generic (already partly RTE) |
| Extra payload framing `faulted | single_mode | cycle | sessions | db | valid` | same, `common_config.h` | framing generic; sessions/db codecs application |
| Decision table, rules 1-7 + readiness gate, actions NONE/PROMOTE/DEMOTE/RENEGOTIATE/ALARM/WAIT | `ab_gp_site_role.c` | generic (readiness input is application) |
| Standby / takeover / tie-break timing predicates | `ab_gp_standby_policy.c` | generic, except `session_adopt_allowed` |
| Promote / demote / renegotiate, apply state transfer, cycle restart policy | `ab_gp_channel_negotiate.c` | trigger generic; what is applied is application |
| Warm standby sync, 10 ms link service tick | same, called from `ab_gp_channel.c`, `ab_gp_channel_checkpoint.c`, `post_execute.c` | generic |
| Takeover confirmation (flag + cycle only, not verified) | same, decoded in `ab_gp_channel.c` | verification generic |
| Fault flush before reboot | same, from `ab_gp_channel_crosscompare.c` | generic |
| A/B role agreement (sibling `{online, single}` compared, 3 mismatches -> reboot) | `ab_gp_channel_crosscompare.c` | generic |
| Readiness (relay frame within 10 cycles), session adoption from sibling, relay gating on STANDBY | `ab_gp_site_status.c`, `pre_execute.c` | application |

What RTE has: `rte_dual_negotiator` (startup tie-break only, no FAULTED state, no promote/demote input, no takeover,
no payload; GP is its only caller), `rte_dual_channel` (transport), `rte_state_transfer` (codec registry, no transport),
`rte_election` (N-way, unused), `rte_redundancy_config` (`standby_mode`). There is no site-role API.

This reverses RBC_GP's decision of 2026-09-23 (review finding A3, "keep both"): under the model, the policy applied to
the negotiator's verdict is platform mechanism, not application concern.

\section platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_decision Decision

A new RTE module `redundancy/site_role/` owns the mechanism; GP becomes its client. The API sketched on 2026-10-02 is
below; the built API differs in names and shape (callbacks in `rte_site_role_config_t` instead of register calls) and
is `include/rte/redundancy/site_role/rte_site_role.h`, summarised in "Service behaviour as built". Sketch:

- `rte_site_role_init(cfg)` - site id, peer id, link (flow) name, `standby_mode`, timers.
- `rte_site_role_register_state(registry)` - GP's `rte_state_transfer` codecs (sessions, db).
- `rte_site_role_register_readiness(fn, ctx)`.
- `rte_site_role_register_on_change(cb)` - new role, reason, result of an applied transfer.
- `rte_site_role_execute(cycle)` (pre-execute stage), `rte_site_role_service()` (fast tick),
  `rte_site_role_sync(cycle)` (before the commit), `rte_site_role_notify_fault()`.
- `rte_site_role_submit_takeover_confirm(bytes, len)` - RTE verifies (authentication, sequence, expiry).
- `rte_site_role_get_role()`, `rte_site_role_get_status()` (the `site_wait` / `site_why` snapshot).

\section platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_built Service behaviour as built (2026-10-07)

Source: `include/rte/redundancy/site_role/rte_site_role.h`, `rte_site_role_policy.h`, `src/redundancy/site_role/`. The
rule list in `rte_site_role_decide()`'s header comment is authoritative. Tests: `tests/site_role/test_rte_site_role.c`,
`tests/site_role/test_rte_site_role_policy.c`.

\subsection platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_built_drive Drive points (option A: the application's cycle)

| Call | When the application calls it | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `rte_site_role_init()` | setup | validates and copies the configuration; opens nothing |
| `rte_site_role_start()` | setup, after `init` | start-up role (below); blocking, bounded by `startup_timeout_ms` |
| `rte_site_role_execute(cycle, local)` | once per cycle (RBC_GP: `pre_execute`) | one site-role step (below) |
| `rte_site_role_service_link(cycle)` | fast tick (RBC_GP: every 10 ms of the tick wait, and inside the checkpoint poll) | drains up to 4 frames, so acknowledgements do not depend on the other site's cycle phase |
| `rte_site_role_sync_standby(cycle, local)` | before the output commit | warm/hot: ONLINE sends this cycle's snapshot and tracks the acknowledgement |
| `rte_site_role_on_takeover_token()` / `_on_takeover_confirm()` | when the dispatcher's confirmation arrives | token check (REQ-SITEROLE-015) / unauthenticated interim path |
| `rte_site_role_note_lost_contact()` | the application stalled while ONLINE | sets the lost-contact history (rule 5) |
| `rte_site_role_flush_faulted()` | right before a fault reboot | best-effort final frame with `faulted = 1` |
| `rte_site_role_sibling_verdict()` | after `execute` | the A/B verdict of that cycle (REQ-SITEROLE-016) |
| `rte_site_role_is_online()`, `_get_status()`, `_takeover_challenge()`, `_get_negotiator_states()` | any time | role, status snapshot (`site_wait` / `site_why` in RBC_GP), current challenge, negotiator diagnostic |

The application supplies: the state codec (`encode_state`, `receive_state`), the change callback (`on_change`, with
`apply_state` on a promotion), the local facts per call (`faulted`, `single_mode`, `own_ready`; the `sibling_*` fields
only without a sibling source), the sibling source, the MAC verifier, and the `trace` / `delay` seams.

\subsection platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_built_start Start-up (`rte_site_role_start()`)

1. Startup timestamp: the configured one (RBC_GP: the value A and B agreed, step 5a) or `rte_timer_now()`. It never
   changes afterwards and is resumed on every reconnect.
2. REQ-SITEROLE-017: the sibling source is polled once. A known frame decides the role (sibling ONLINE -> ONLINE,
   otherwise STANDBY, negotiator seeded with `resume_own_state`); no frame or no source -> step 4 decides.
3. Link: the listen side opens once; the connect side retries every `connect_retry_ms` (each attempt
   `connect_timeout_ms`) up to `startup_timeout_ms`. Then `rte_dual_channel` (resync on sequence error) and
   `rte_dual_negotiator` are initialised.
4. Tie-break (REQ-DUAL-NEGOTIATOR-003): `rte_dual_negotiator_execute(link_poll_ms)` rounds until this side leaves IDLE;
   the older startup timestamp wins ONLINE, an exact tie goes to the smaller id. A resumed role is not IDLE, so one round
   ends the loop.
5. Not settled within `startup_timeout_ms`: link closed, `RTE_STATUS_TIMEOUT` (RBC_GP treats it as a fatal setup step).
   Known open defect: zero timing values loop for ever and the real bound exceeds `startup_timeout_ms` (ISS-042).

\subsection platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_built_execute One `rte_site_role_execute()`

1. Take the local facts; poll the sibling source (a fresh frame is stamped with this cycle); refresh the takeover
   challenge.
2. Link down: one reconnect attempt bounded by `reconnect_attempt_ms` (REQ-SITEROLE-012), resuming the startup
   timestamp and, when ONLINE, the ONLINE state. Failed: the decision and the A/B check still run (rule 4 needs no
   link), then return.
3. Negotiator beacon, non-blocking (diagnostic after start-up: the role is never taken from it again).
4. Send the frame `faulted(1) | single_mode(1) | cycle(4, LE) | state | valid(1)` (live state, the cycle and
   `valid = 0xA5` only while ONLINE); receive the counterpart's latest frame. "Valid and not faulted" = the counterpart
   is ONLINE now; a valid frame is kept as the snapshot to adopt on promotion; any decoded frame clears
   `peer_unresponsive` and a pending confirmation. The received length is not checked (ISS-038).
5. Warm/hot: a snapshot silent for more than 3 cycles is dropped (`RTE_SITE_ROLE_STANDBY_DATA_TIMEOUT_CYCLES`).
6. Decision: `rte_site_role_decide()`, one action applied (REQ-SITEROLE-011), every change reported through `on_change`.
7. A/B check (with a source): `rte_site_role_check_sibling()` against the role just decided.
8. Supervision (REQ-SITEROLE-013): `send_miss_threshold` consecutive unacknowledged sends, or a receive error other
   than a timeout, close the link. ONLINE: lost-contact history set. STANDBY: `peer_unresponsive` set, `TAKEOVER`
   warning, the channel stays STANDBY.

\subsection platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_built_rules Decision rules (summary of `rte_site_role_decide()`)

First match wins (REQ-SITEROLE-001). "Answering" = a counterpart frame within the last 2 cycles.

| # | Condition | Action |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | own and counterpart FAULTED | RENEGOTIATE: WEST ONLINE, EAST STANDBY |
| 2 | STANDBY, not FAULTED, counterpart FAULTED | PROMOTE |
| 3 | own FAULTED, counterpart healthy, valid counterpart snapshot held | DEMOTE |
| 4 | STANDBY, not FAULTED, counterpart unresponsive, dispatcher confirmation at most `RTE_SITE_ROLE_TAKEOVER_CONFIRM_VALID_CYCLES` (120) old | PROMOTE |
| 5 | ONLINE, nobody FAULTED, counterpart ONLINE now and answering | DEMOTE if lost-contact history (yield), else ALARM (dual ONLINE, not resolved) |
| 6 | STANDBY, link up, counterpart answering, responsive, not ONLINE, not FAULTED, for `RTE_SITE_ROLE_BOTH_STANDBY_CYCLES` (6) consecutive cycles on WEST, or 1 cycle when the A/B sibling is ONLINE | PROMOTE |
| 7 | STANDBY, A/B sibling ONLINE, counterpart not FAULTED and not (ONLINE now and answering), for 2 consecutive cycles | PROMOTE (follow the sibling) |

Readiness gate (REQ-SITEROLE-002): a PROMOTE becomes WAIT unless `own_ready` and the sibling is ONLINE, ready or not
reachable. Silence alone never promotes (REQ-SITEROLE-003). A restarted cycle counter makes a confirmation expired and
a snapshot stale (REQ-SITEROLE-004).

\subsection platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_built_sibling A/B role agreement (REQ-SITEROLE-014/016)

- With `sibling_source` set, the service polls it once per `execute` (and once in `start`). Sibling online / ready =
  the last known frame; reachable = a frame within `sibling_reach_cycles` (0 = 3) cycles; a cycle number that went back
  makes the frame unreachable. These replace the `sibling_*` local facts.
- After the decision, `{online, single_mode}` of this channel is compared with the **last known** sibling frame
  (reachability does not gate the check): no frame -> UNKNOWN; equal -> AGREE (count reset); different -> PENDING, at
  `RTE_SITE_ROLE_SIBLING_MISMATCH_LIMIT` (3) consecutive -> FAULT, with the `NEGOTIATION_MISMATCH` ERROR event. The
  application reads `rte_site_role_sibling_verdict()` and owns the reaction (RBC_GP: reboot).
- Without a source the application supplies the sibling facts and calls `rte_site_role_check_sibling()` itself (the
  step 3 path; RBC_GP no longer uses it).

\subsection platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_built_token Takeover confirmation (REQ-SITEROLE-015)

With `verify_mac` set: a 24-byte token (16-byte message `version | site id | channel id | 0 | challenge | issuer
sequence | 0`, 8-byte MAC) is accepted only for this channel, with a MAC the verifier accepts and the current
challenge. The challenge is drawn with `rte_random`, replaced after each accepted token and after
`challenge_lifetime_cycles` (0 = 240); no challenge, no token. While ONLINE a token is ignored. The unauthenticated
`rte_site_role_on_takeover_confirm()` is refused when a verifier is configured. An accepted confirmation is valid for
120 cycles and only acts through rule 4.

\subsection platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_built_open Known open defects of the service

ISS-036 (the negotiator and the dual channel ignore a failed `rte_timer_now()`; the watchdog part is fixed), ISS-038
(received frame length not checked), ISS-042 (start-up bound), ISS-043 (dual channel acknowledges then overwrites a
DATA frame; benign for the one-link site role). All in the root `ISSUES.dox`.

\section platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_steps Steps

Each step keeps behaviour identical and is verified by the existing unit tests, the Docker `2oo2_tests` and
`fault_injection` suites, and `smoke.sh` before the next one starts.

1. **Pure parts first.** Move the decision table (`ab_gp_site_role_decide`, its input/output types and reason tokens)
   and the generic standby predicates into `rte_site_role_policy` with the decision-table test ported unchanged.
   GP calls the RTE functions. No timing or wire change.
2. **Mechanism.** Move link ownership, the negotiator stacking, payload framing, promote/demote/renegotiate, warm sync
   and the fault flush behind the API above, still driven from GP's existing call points (pre-execute, the 10 ms tick,
   pre-commit). GP registers codecs, readiness and the change callback. Wire layout and log strings unchanged (the
   Robot suites match on them).
3. **A/B agreement.** The sibling role check moves into RTE; GP forwards the sibling site-state bytes until RTE owns
   the peer link.
4. **Takeover verification.** Authenticated confirmation with sequence and expiry, keyed like the other verified
   inputs; the trigger side is the CMI module of RBC_GA_NCBIR (gap G5). Until then the unauthenticated interim file
   stays and is labelled as such.
5. **Transport on `rte_flow`** (with Phase 2b / gap G3), then delete the GP copies and update RBC_GP `CLAUDE.md`
   (the A3 decision) and `rte_redundancy_config.h` (its `standby_mode` comment is out of date).

\section platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_open Open decisions

1. **Decided 2026-10-02:** the model overrides the A3 "keep both" decision.
2. **Decided 2026-10-02: option A** - the drive points stay in the application's cycle (today's behaviour), no RTE
   site-link thread.
3. **Taken as proposed 2026-10-03 (step 3):** GP forwards the sibling state until step 5 puts the peer link in RTE.
   Since step 5b (5b-1, 5b-2) the service polls a sibling source GP supplies and runs the check itself; GP still owns
   the A/B peer link until step 5c.
4. **Decided 2026-10-03:** trust model for the takeover confirmation. A symmetric MAC - the Subset-037 MAC of
   Platform_Protocol_SafeComm (`mac/`), already used on the train link - with one takeover key per site. RTE does
   not link a crypto library: the application passes a `verify_mac` callback. The token is a 16-byte message
   (version, site id, channel id, the channel's challenge, an issuer sequence for the audit trail) and its 8-byte
   MAC. Freshness is challenge-response, not a wall clock (`rte_clocksync` must not feed a vital decision): each
   channel draws a random challenge (`rte_random`), publishes it in its status line, and accepts only a token for the
   current one; the challenge is replaced after each accepted token (single use) and after
   `challenge_lifetime_cycles` (the expiry). A token is per channel, so A and B each verify their own. The interim
   issuer is RBC_Maintenance_Web (mTLS login, audited) through the status Gateway; the target issuer is the CMI module
   of RBC_GA_NCBIR (gap G5). **Known limit:** until CMI issues tokens the key sits on the Gateway (non-vital), so a
   compromised Gateway can confirm a takeover; the promotion rules still apply (the counterpart must be unresponsive),
   so it cannot create two ONLINE sites while the counterpart answers. Without a configured verifier the
   unauthenticated path stays and is logged as `unauthenticated`.

\section platformrte_docs_architecture_ADR_040_site_role_service_risks Risks

- SIL 4 promotion logic: the table semantics (rules 1-7, WAIT) and the A/B agreement must stay in step, or every change
  risks a `NEGOTIATION_MISMATCH` reboot.
- The negotiation runs inside the cycle, the 10 ms tick and the checkpoint loop; moving the drive points changes timing.
- Wire layout and log strings are used by the Robot suites and `smoke.sh`.
- Resolved by step 5a (2026-10-06): A and B shared the startup timestamp through a `/tmp` file, which did not work across
  separate hosts; it is now a `STARTUP_TS` exchange over the A/B peer link (RBC_GP).
*/
